# The national unit for russia. Items take n=3; the world items reviewed here change by level.
# Dates are in engine/notation/chronicle.ts with their sources.
lesson history-g5-russia-our-country-in-its-time v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=9 subject=history nation=russia levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Letters on birch bark"
  goal "Put the story of Novgorod's birch-bark letters in order, and say why letters by ordinary people tell us so much."

  look {
    scene 36x9 {
      timeline line from=800 to=2000 step=200 years=[859, 1240, 1951] labels=["Novgorod first mentioned", "Onfim's homework", "Birch-bark letters found"] at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      Novgorod is first named in a chronicle under the year 859. Its people wrote notes and letters by scratching them on strips of birch bark. On 26 July 1951 archaeologists found the first one, kept safe in the wet clay. More than a thousand have been found since, among them the homework and drawings of a boy called Onfim, who lived about 1220 to 1260.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.n5-what n=3
    }
    show history.n5-order n=3 k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.n5-between n=3 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.n5-century n=3 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.voyage-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.continent-letter k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.print-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.n5-order n=3 k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=0
    level medium hard {
      show history.n5-what n=3
    }
    level easy {
      show history.n5-century n=3 k=1
    }
    level hard {
      show history.voyage-order k=5
    }
  }
  story {
    show history.n5-between n=3 k=1 hard-k=0
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.n5-why n=3
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.n5-find-out n=3
  }
  remember {
    say "Every date in our country's story can be put on the line and counted from, and every one came from a source."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is to use the grade's methods (timelines across year one, centuries, sources) on a
    chapter of the family's own country. The way in names what a thing was; the core orders three
    events, counts the years between two, names a century, and reviews voyages, continents and
    printing; the two-star question asks why something was done. If the child is stuck on
    counting, count to the next hundred first. The three-star task is for you to mark: a small
    piece of finding out with you. Russia's history programme for grade six includes «Распространение грамотности, берестяные грамоты», the spread of reading and writing and the birch-bark letters.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example says what the thing was, the events come nearly in order, the years counted are the closest pair, and the review questions are the plainest."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the events are fully muddled, the years counted are the longer pair, the century asked has a trap, and the review questions cross year one."
  }
}
